WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.200
Cross-platform development often carries a stigma of sluggishness.

2
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:06.100
Look at these two interactions.

3
00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:09.700
The card on the left updates at 120 frames per second.

4
00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:13.100
The one on the right, stutters, and drops frames mid-drag.

5
00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:15.800
Both interfaces are built in React Native.

6
00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:18.700
The difference lies in where the calculation happens.

7
00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:23.100
High-frequency interaction logic needs to live outside the JavaScript thread.

8
00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:26.200
The JavaScript thread acts as a single-lane highway.

9
00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:28.800
When it's parsing network data or business logic,

10
00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:32.400
UI updates wait and traffic, causing visible hitches.

11
00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.400
We can address this by mapping the React Native architecture

12
00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:37.900
onto the iOS core animation model.

13
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:40.800
This requires tracing the Native iOS rendering pipeline

14
00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.200
to find its cross-platform equivalents.

15
00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:45.300
We will look at how JavaScript can be compiled

16
00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.700
to run alongside Native components.

17
00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:50.700
Native level performance relies on decoupling visual state

18
00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:52.500
from main thread execution.

19
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:55.300
This diagram shows the iOS division of labor.

20
00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:57.500
The top track represents the main thread,

21
00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:59.600
where UI and logic live.

22
00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:03.700
The bottom track is the render server dedicated to drawing pixels.

23
00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:08.500
Standard operations like uiv.animate are tied to the top track.

24
00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:11.900
If the main thread gets blocked, those animations freeze.

25
00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:13.900
Core animation operates differently,

26
00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:16.100
handing instructions down to the render server

27
00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:17.900
where the animation loops natively,

28
00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:20.100
surviving main thread blockages.

29
00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:23.600
In React Native, developers use the built-in animated API

30
00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:25.100
to replicate this bridge.

31
00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:27.900
The use native driver flag access the bridge.

32
00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:29.600
Before the first frame renders,

33
00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:33.100
this flag serializes a fixed set of animation instructions

34
00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:35.000
and hands them to the native side.

35
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:37.600
The payload runs independently on the UI thread,

36
00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:39.700
but this static handoff has a limit.

37
00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:42.200
It only supports layout independent properties.

38
00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:44.500
Opacity and transform are accepted,

39
00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:46.600
while width and height are rejected.

40
00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:50.600
Modifying a views geometry requires active layout recalculation.

41
00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:53.700
A static pre-serialized payload cannot compute

42
00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:55.300
those changes on the fly.

43
00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:57.800
On native iOS, cab basic animation

44
00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:00.700
handles these complexities without jumping threads.

45
00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:05.100
The built-in animated API functions as a fire and forget mechanism.

46
00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:06.600
It stops working when dynamic,

47
00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:09.400
continuous layout calculations are required.

48
00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:11.900
React Native reanimated version 3 provides

49
00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:13.800
the architectural evolution required

50
00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.400
to move past static serialization.

51
00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:18.300
This diagram shows workloads.

52
00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:20.300
Instead of sending static descriptions,

53
00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:22.500
JavaScript functions are compiled directly

54
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:24.600
into native US thread logic.

55
00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:27.500
A babel plugin detects workloads and re-writes them.

56
00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:30.300
They execute in an isolated loop on the bottom track,

57
00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:32.300
separate from main thread traffic.

58
00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:34.400
This loop is driven by the hardware clock,

59
00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.300
syncing with refresh intervals.

60
00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:38.800
This maps to a CAD display link callback,

61
00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:41.200
executing Swift logic on the render side.

62
00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:43.900
The developer writes the aneration logic in JavaScript,

63
00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:46.600
but at runtime, those instructions perfectly execute

64
00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:47.700
as native code.

65
00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:50.000
Because the logic executes natively,

66
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.300
dynamic layout recalculations happen in place.

67
00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:55.700
The system does not have to ask the JavaScript thread

68
00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:57.500
for new coordinate data.

69
00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:00.800
Reanimated elevates JavaScript from a distant orchestrator

70
00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:04.200
into a citizen operating on the native UI thread.

71
00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:05.500
To drive these workloads,

72
00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:07.500
the architecture requires state management

73
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:10.100
that exists on that same native thread.

74
00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:12.400
This is handled by you shared value,

75
00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:16.400
the equivalent of an iOS at state property on the render side.

76
00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:19.300
Reads and writes inside a workload are synchronous.

77
00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:21.900
If the JavaScript thread reads that same value,

78
00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:25.400
the request is handled asynchronously to prevent blocking.

79
00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:27.400
This chart visualizes a physics curve

80
00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:29.800
powered by that shared native state.

81
00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:33.900
The width spring function maps to CA spring animation in iOS,

82
00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:36.700
giving you control over stiffness, damping, mass,

83
00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:38.500
and initial velocity.

84
00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:41.000
Rather than tweening over a fixed duration,

85
00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:43.100
the system calculates the physical trajectory

86
00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:44.500
based on those characteristics,

87
00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:46.200
resolving the motion locally.

88
00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:48.600
This execution speed allows the software

89
00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:50.700
to utilize the high fidelity display

90
00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:53.100
of the iPhone 13 Pro hardware.

91
00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:54.500
On promotion devices,

92
00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:57.600
reanimated utilizes the fabric architecture.

93
00:03:57.600 --> 00:03:59.600
Worklets sync with CAD display link

94
00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:02.300
to run at 120 frames per second.

95
00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:03.500
Synchronous shared state

96
00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:06.100
and localized physics equations allow react native

97
00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:09.700
to hit 120 hertz without explicit configuration.

98
00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:11.500
User interactivity is captured

99
00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:14.300
using the React Native gesture handler system.

100
00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:15.400
This library operates

101
00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:19.200
like UI gesture recognizer subclasses in native iOS.

102
00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:22.400
This diagram tracks touch data as it is processed natively,

103
00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:24.900
which allows the system to bypass the JavaScript node

104
00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:28.000
and push updates into UI thread shared values.

105
00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:30.900
The interaction callbacks begin, update, and end

106
00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:32.200
are workloads.

107
00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:34.500
Dragging an element creates a closed update loop

108
00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:37.300
that leaves the main JavaScript thread undisturbed.

109
00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:39.700
Frame-by-frame rendering for complex visual effects

110
00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:41.800
requires deeper graphical access.

111
00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:44.100
This is where React Native Skiya comes in.

112
00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:45.600
It serves the same purpose

113
00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:49.600
as drawing in a K-layer subclass via CG context.

114
00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:52.100
Skiya provides a 2D drawing interface

115
00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:53.600
that runs on the UI thread

116
00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:56.300
and integrates with reanimated state.

117
00:04:56.300 --> 00:05:00.300
This visualization shows the Skiya Canvas rendering frame-by-frame,

118
00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:03.100
pulling data synchronously from native shared values

119
00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:05.300
and outputting to the device hardware.

120
00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:07.500
Paths, gradients, and blur effects

121
00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:10.500
are hardware accelerated through the Metal Graphics API

122
00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:13.100
just like native core image pipelines.

123
00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:16.000
By coupling native gestures to metal-backed drawing,

124
00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:18.900
you construct an isolated, high-performance rendering loop

125
00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:21.200
governed by JavaScript syntax.

126
00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:23.300
Theoretical architecture must be proven

127
00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:25.100
through runtime diagnostics.

128
00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:27.200
When an architectural migration fails,

129
00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:31.000
animation stutter when screen navigation logic hits the main thread.

130
00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:33.900
To verify that an animation is running on the UI thread,

131
00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:35.700
we compare the Hermes profiler,

132
00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:37.500
which tracks JavaScript activity

133
00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:41.300
with Apple's instruments, which tracks core animation layer updates.

134
00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:43.900
An optimized reanimated implementation

135
00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:46.100
will show zero JavaScript activity

136
00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:48.100
after the gesture initiates.

137
00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:52.500
The computational workload appears natively on the render server trace.

138
00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:56.200
This matrix synthesizes the translation ecosystem.

139
00:05:56.200 --> 00:06:01.000
Standard uiv.animate translates to the built-in native driver.

140
00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:05.200
CA spring animation translates to the with spring function

141
00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:07.400
and CA display link synchronization

142
00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:09.500
is achieved using workloads.

143
00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:13.600
The native at-state property is handled by use-shared value

144
00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:18.000
and CALA are drawing is matched by react native skia.

145
00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:21.300
The perceived performance gap between cross-platform logic

146
00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:23.800
and native rendering is an illusion.

147
00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:26.300
Reanimated provides the compiler necessary

148
00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:28.800
to speak the render server's native language.

